.TH SOURCEKNOCK-KNOCK 1 "2026-11-02" "sourceknock 1.0" "sourceknock"
.SH NAME
sourceknock-knock \- send a source-port knock to a sourceknock server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sourceknock-knock
.RB [ \-p
.IR port ]
.RB [ \-d
.IR ms ]
.RB [ \-s
.IR addr ]
.RB [ \-4 | \-6 ]
.I host
.I sequence
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sourceknock-knock
sends one UDP datagram per port in
.IR sequence ,
in order, each bound to that port as its
.B source
port, to
.IR host .
The
.BR sourceknock (1)
daemon reads the knock from the source ports. The destination port only
matters if the trigger sets
.BR knock_port ;
pass it with
.BR \-p .
.PP
Each datagram is sent from its own socket, so the knock steps leave in the
order given, with
.I ms
milliseconds between them.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-p " port"
Destination port (default 9). Use the trigger's
.B knock_port
if it sets one.
.TP
.BI \-d " ms"
Delay between knock steps in milliseconds (default 50). Keep the whole knock
inside the trigger's
.BR window .
.TP
.BI \-s " addr"
Local address to send from (default: any).
.TP
.BR \-4 ", " \-6
Use IPv4 or IPv6 only.
.TP
.B \-h
Show usage.
.SH EXAMPLES
Knock 7000, 8000, 9000, then connect:
.IP
.nf
sourceknock-knock server.example.com 7000,8000,9000
ssh user@server.example.com
.fi
.PP
A trigger with
.B knock_port = 4444
over IPv6:
.IP
.nf
sourceknock-knock -6 -p 4444 server.example.com 7000,8000,9000
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All datagrams were sent.
.TP
.B 1
Name resolution, bind or send failed (for example the source port is in use,
or is below 1024 and you are not root).
.TP
.B 2
Invalid arguments.
.SH NOTES
NAT can rewrite source ports. Home routers usually keep them; carrier-grade
and mobile NAT usually do not, and then no knock can arrive intact. See the
README's "NAT" section for how to check.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sourceknock (1)
